{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba skills/mission/SKILL.md | sed -n '1,220p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t---\n     2\tname: mission\n     3\tdescription: Start an autonomous Darkfactory mission. Grills the user for alignment, creates OpenSpec artifacts, writes a contract, writes the mission directory, dispatches workers serially, validates each task against the contract, runs fix loops with circuit breakers, and creates a draft PR on completion.\n     4\tflow: align -> spec -> contract -> build -> validate -> ship\n     5\t---\n     6\t\n     7\t## Rules (Always Enforced)\n     8\t\n     9\t1. **Serial execution.** One worker at a time. Never dispatch two workers in parallel. (Exception: the two adversarial reviewers in Phase 9 run in parallel since they are independent read-only reviewers.)\n    10\t2. **Adversarial separation.** Workers never see `contract.md`. Validators never see `proposal.md` or `design.md`.\n    11\t3. **Both per-task validators re-run after every fix.** Even if only one failed, both scrutiny and user-test re-run to catch regressions. Exception: Phase 9 gate fixes run scrutiny only. Adversarial review runs once at the end, not per task.\n    12\t4. **Circuit breaker at 3.** After 3 failed attempts on the same task, stop and ask the human. Retry allows one more attempt but does NOT reset the counter.\n    13\t5. **File-backed state.** All decisions and status go to disk. The mission must be resumable from files alone.\n    14\t6. **Single commit per task.** One task, one worker, one commit, one validation cycle.\n    15\t7. **Draft PRs only.** Never create a ready-for-review PR. The human promotes it.\n    16\t8. **Plan is the operating manual.** Keep the plan updated after every phase and step. It must be accurate if context compacts.\n    17\t9. **Skipped tasks use `- [~]`.** The build loop treats `- [~]` as neither unchecked nor complete. Skipped tasks are not re-attempted.\n    18\t10. **Mark tasks done in tasks.md immediately.** After a task passes validation, change `- [ ]` to `- [x]` in tasks.md before moving to the next task. This is required for resume to work correctly.\n    19\t\n    20\t---\n    21\t\n    22\t## Phase Index\n    23\t\n    24\t0. **Enter Plan Mode** -- write the durable operating plan\n    25\t1. **Grill for Alignment** -- align on what is being built and why\n    26\t2. **Establish Shared Language** -- create/update UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md\n    27\t3. **Write a PRD** -- file requirements as a GitHub issue\n    28\t4. **Create and Review OpenSpec Artifacts** -- proposal, specs, design, tasks (human-approved)\n    29\t4a. **Spec Coverage Audit** -- verify every PRD requirement maps to a task\n    30\t5. **Write Contract** -- validation assertions workers never see\n    31\t6. **Create Mission Directory** -- set up mission/ state directory\n    32\t7. **Launch Confirmation** -- human confirms, build starts\n    33\t8. **Build Loop** -- dispatch workers, validate per task, fix loop with circuit breaker\n    34\t9. **Mission Completion** -- final gates, spec delta check, draft PR, dual adversarial review\n    35\t\n    36\t---\n    37\t\n    38\t## Phase 0: Enter Plan Mode\n    39\t\n    40\t> **Rules:** 5, 8\n    41\t> **Goal:** Write the durable operating plan that survives context compaction.\n    42\t> **Inputs:** User's idea (from conversation), UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md (if exists)\n    43\t> **Outputs:** Plan (in plan mode), UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md confirmed read or noted absent\n    44\t\n    45\tRun `/mission-init` to verify that all Darkfactory skills are installed and required CLI tools are available. If `/mission-init` reports any issues, resolve them before proceeding.\n    46\t\n    47\tEnter plan mode immediately. Write a durable operating manual as the plan. This plan survives context compaction and is the orchestrator's source of truth.\n    48\t\n    49\t### Plan Template\n    50\t\n    51\tWrite this plan, filling in values as they become known. Read the plan structure from [templates/plan.md](templates/plan.md).\n    52\t\n    53\tTo fill in the **Darkfactory SHA** field, run `git -C ~/.darkfactory rev-parse HEAD`; if `git -C ~/.darkfactory status --porcelain` returns anything, append `-dirty` to the SHA. Paste the result into the plan. If `~/.darkfactory` does not exist (non-standard install), resolve the darkfactory clone path from the symlink target of `~/.claude/skills/mission` and use that instead.\n    54\t\n    55\t### First Action\n    56\t\n    57\tCheck if `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` exists in the repo root. If it does, read it now so all phases use consistent terminology.\n    58\t\n    59\t**Gate -- proceed when:**\n    60\t- [ ] `/mission-init` passed (all skills symlinked, CLI tools present)\n    61\t- [ ] Plan is written and active in plan mode\n    62\t- [ ] Darkfactory SHA captured in plan\n    63\t- [ ] UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md checked (read if exists, noted if absent)\n    64\t\n    65\t---\n    66\t\n    67\t## Phase 1: Grill for Alignment\n    68\t\n    69\t> **Rules:** --\n    70\t> **Goal:** Align on what is being built, why, and what \"done\" looks like.\n    71\t> **Inputs:** User's idea (from conversation)\n    72\t> **Outputs:** Grill conversation (in context, used by Phases 2 and 3), key decisions summarized in plan\n    73\t\n    74\tRun `/grill` to interview the user about their idea. The grill session should drive toward shared understanding of what is being built, why, and what \"done\" looks like.\n    75\t\n    76\tIf the user says \"skip\":\n    77\t- Warn: \"Skipping the grill means the contract will be based on whatever I can infer. Contract quality depends on alignment quality. Are you sure?\"\n    78\t- If they confirm, proceed to Phase 2.\n    79\t\n    80\tAfter the grill completes, summarize the key decisions in the plan.\n    81\t\n    82\t**Gate -- proceed when:**\n    83\t- [ ] Grill complete or explicitly skipped (with warning acknowledged)\n    84\t- [ ] Key decisions summarized in plan\n    85\t\n    86\t---\n    87\t\n    88\t## Phase 2: Establish Shared Language\n    89\t\n    90\t> **Rules:** 5\n    91\t> **Goal:** Establish shared terminology for all subsequent phases.\n    92\t> **Inputs:** Grill conversation (from Phase 1, in context), UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md (if exists from Phase 0 check)\n    93\t> **Outputs:** UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md (created or updated)\n    94\t\n    95\tRun `/language` to create or update `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md`.\n    96\t\n    97\t- If the file already existed and was read in Phase 0, this phase updates it with any new terms from the grill session.\n    98\t- If the file did not exist, this phase creates it. Read the file after creation so all subsequent phases use the terms.\n    99\t\n   100\t**Gate -- proceed when:**\n   101\t- [ ] UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md exists and is loaded into context\n   102\t\n   103\t---\n   104\t\n   105\t## Phase 3: Write a PRD\n   106\t\n   107\t> **Rules:** 5\n   108\t> **Goal:** Capture requirements as a structured PRD filed as a GitHub issue.\n   109\t> **Inputs:** Grill conversation (from Phase 1, in context), UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md\n   110\t> **Outputs:** GitHub issue (PRD), issue URL + number recorded in plan\n   111\t\n   112\tRun `/prd` to turn the grill conversation into a structured product requirements document. Use terms from `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` consistently throughout the PRD. The PRD captures: problem statement, solution, user stories, implementation decisions, testing decisions, and out-of-scope items.\n   113\t\n   114\tAfter the issue is created, record both the issue URL and the issue number in the plan (under Spec Coverage Audit). The issue number is needed in Phase 9 to link the draft PR back to the issue.\n   115\t\n   116\t**Gate -- proceed when:**\n   117\t- [ ] PRD filed as GitHub issue\n   118\t- [ ] Issue URL and issue number recorded in plan\n   119\t\n   120\t---\n   121\t\n   122\t## Phase 4: Create and Review OpenSpec Artifacts\n   123\t\n   124\t> **Rules:** 5\n   125\t> **Goal:** Generate and get human approval on all four OpenSpec artifacts.\n   126\t> **Inputs:** PRD (read from GitHub issue URL in plan), UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md\n   127\t> **Outputs:** proposal.md, specs/, design.md, tasks.md -- all human-approved\n   128\t\n   129\tRead the PRD from the GitHub issue URL recorded in the plan. This is the source of truth for all artifact generation. Use terms from `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` consistently in all artifacts.\n   130\t\n   131\tThe `spec-driven` schema generates four artifacts in dependency order: `proposal -> specs -> design -> tasks`. Generate each artifact one at a time, present it to the human for review, and get approval before generating the next.\n   132\t\n   133\t**Do NOT run `/opsx:propose` as a single pass.** Instead, orchestrate artifact creation using the openspec CLI commands directly:\n   134\t\n   135\t1. `openspec new change \"<name>\"` -- create the change directory\n   136\t2. `openspec status --change \"<name>\" --json` -- get artifact order and dependencies\n   137\t3. For each artifact in order:\n   138\t   a. `openspec instructions <artifact-id> --change \"<name>\" --json` -- get instructions for this artifact\n   139\t   b. Create the artifact file following the instructions and template\n   140\t   c. `openspec status --change \"<name>\" --json` -- re-run status to refresh the artifact list (newly unblocked artifacts and per-capability spec IDs appear only after their dependencies are written)\n   141\t\n   142\t### Review Pattern (used at every review point in planning)\n   143\t\n   144\tEvery artifact review follows this pattern:\n   145\t1. **AI presents** -- show the artifact content to the human in the chat\n   146\t2. **AI recommends** -- state whether it looks good, flag any concerns, suggest specific changes if needed\n   147\t3. **Human decides** -- approve, request changes, or defer to the AI recommendation\n   148\t\n   149\tThe human can always say \"looks good\" or \"go with your recommendation\" to keep momentum. If changes are requested, revise and re-present.\n   150\t\n   151\t### Phase 4.1: Generate and Review Proposal\n   152\t\n   153\tGenerate `proposal.md` (what and why). This establishes the capabilities that will become spec files.\n   154\t\n   155\tPresent it to the human with a recommendation. If approved, proceed to Phase 4.2.\n   156\t\n   157\t### Phase 4.2: Generate and Review Specs\n   158\t\n   159\tGenerate `specs/<capability>/spec.md` files (one per capability listed in the proposal). Each spec contains formal WHEN/THEN requirements using SHALL/MUST language.\n   160\t\n   161\tPresent all spec files to the human with a recommendation. Specs are the formal requirements that flow into tasks and the contract. Missing or vague specs are the primary cause of missed requirements in the final build.\n   162\t\n   163\tIf approved, proceed to Phase 4.3.\n   164\t\n   165\t### Phase 4.3: Generate and Review Design\n   166\t\n   167\tGenerate `design.md` (how -- architecture decisions, alternatives considered, risks). Reference the proposal for motivation and specs for requirements.\n   168\t\n   169\tDesign is required for every mission. The build loop and worker briefs depend on design.md existing -- skipping it would break downstream phases.\n   170\t\n   171\t#### Module Depth\n   172\t\n   173\tFor each major component or module in the design, consider its depth. A deep module has a small interface hiding significant implementation. A shallow module has a large interface with thin logic behind it. Prefer deep modules -- they are more testable at the boundary, easier for workers to implement in a single task, and easier for subsequent workers to use correctly.\n   174\t\n   175\tFor each module, describe:\n   176\t- **Interface** -- what callers see (public API, CLI flags, function signatures)\n   177\t- **What it hides** -- the complexity behind the interface that callers don't need to know about\n   178\t- **Why this boundary** -- what makes this the right place to draw the line\n   179\t\n   180\tIf a module has a large interface relative to its implementation, ask whether it should be split differently or whether some of its interface belongs in a higher-level module. A good test: can a worker implement this module in a single task and a validator test it at the boundary? If not, the module may be too shallow or too broad.\n   181\t\n   182\t#### Existing Codebases\n   183\t\n   184\tWhen the mission modifies an existing codebase (not greenfield), the design must evaluate the current code structure against the planned architecture. For each module the design touches, read the existing code and assess whether it aligns with the target design. If existing modules are shallow, tightly coupled, or conflict with the planned architecture, the design should call this out explicitly and recommend refactoring as part of the implementation. These refactoring decisions flow naturally into task ordering in Phase 4.4 -- refactoring tasks go before the feature tasks that depend on them.\n   185\t\n   186\tPresent it to the human with a recommendation. If approved, proceed to Phase 4.4.\n   187\t\n   188\t### Phase 4.4: Generate and Review Tasks\n   189\t\n   190\tGenerate `tasks.md` (ordered checklist of implementation tasks).\n   191\t\n   192\t#### Task Quality Standard\n   193\t\n   194\tEach task must be a **vertical slice**: a thin end-to-end path through all layers (schema, API, UI, tests) that delivers narrow but complete functionality.\n   195\t\n   196\t**One task = one worker = one commit = one validation cycle.**\n   197\t\n   198\tA task is good if you can demo it and verify it independently after it lands.\n   199\t\n   200\tWhen a task adds public surface area to a module (new exports, API endpoints, CLI flags), include the implementation behind that surface in the same task. Do not split a module's interface from its implementation across tasks -- the per-task validator judges depth from a single diff and will flag an interface without the work behind it.\n   201\t\n   202\t#### Mandatory Closing Tasks\n   203\t\n   204\tEvery mission's tasks.md MUST include these closing tasks after all feature tasks:\n   205\t\n   206\t1. **Smoke test (mandatory).** Write or update one end-to-end test that runs the project's primary user flow against real dependencies. No project-internal mocks. Place it where the project's standard test command runs it. If the mission produces no runnable user flow, note that in the handoff.\n   207\t\n   208\t2. **Cleanup task (mandatory).** Remove unused dependencies, dead code, leftover scaffolding, temporary artifacts, and any backwards-compatibility shims. Workers sometimes leave debris that per-task validation does not catch. This task cleans up the codebase before the shape re-check so the re-check does not flag soon-to-be-deleted surface area.\n   209\t\n   210\t3. **Module shape re-check (mandatory for missions with 5+ feature tasks; optional otherwise).** Re-read design.md's module descriptions. For each module, compare the description to what the code actually exposes -- in whatever form is natural for the project (public functions, React props, HTTP endpoints, CLI flags, exported types, data schemas, message topics, job parameters, config keys, whatever contract callers actually depend on). If reality has drifted meaningfully from the description, either update design.md to match (deliberate change) or refactor the code back (unintended drift). \"Meaningful drift\" means: any public surface item added, removed, or renamed that is not reflected in design.md; any change to the vocabulary or contract callers depend on; or a cumulative widening where a module that was described as having N operations now has noticeably more. Temporary shims and internal helpers do not count. For small missions (under 5 feature tasks) a one-line \"no drift observed\" note in the handoff is sufficient -- this step exists to catch cumulative drift across many tasks, which rarely occurs in short missions. This catches drift that per-task scrutiny cannot see.\n   211\t\n   212\t   Write the findings to `mission/validations/module-shape-audit.md` -- a short free-form note listing each module, whether drift was found, and what was done about it (\"no drift,\" \"design.md updated,\" or \"refactored\"). No required format; the file exists as a record that the check happened.\n   213\t\n   214\t4. **Documentation task (mandatory).** Write or update documentation for what was built. The orchestrator recommends the documentation scope based on what the feature tasks produce -- this varies by mission type. Not every mission needs install instructions, architecture diagrams, or API docs. Use terms from `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` consistently. Any commands shown in docs must be executed against the built artifacts and the actual output pasted -- don't show output that wasn't produced. If unsure what documentation is needed, ask the human during this review.\n   215\t\n   216\t5. **CI configuration task (optional).** Add or update CI/CD pipeline configuration. The orchestrator recommends whether to include this based on the change scope. The human decides during this review.\n   217\t\n   218\tIf the design (Phase 4.3) identified refactoring needed for existing code, those tasks go before the feature tasks in tasks.md.\n   219\t\n   220\tPresent the full task list (including closing tasks) to the human with a recommendation. If approved, proceed to Phase 4a.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
