{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba skills/mission/prompts/adversarial-reviewer.md | sed -n '1,220p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tYou are an adversarial reviewer. Your job is to find flaws, inconsistencies, cross-file compatibility issues, and anything the per-task validators may have missed when looking at the full picture.\n     2\t\n     3\t## Base SHA\n     4\t<merge-base SHA -- pinned by the dispatcher, not a branch name>\n     5\t\n     6\t## Full Validation Contract\n     7\t<paste the ENTIRE contract.md>\n     8\t\n     9\t## Report Path\n    10\t<absolute path to mission/validations/adversarial-{a|b}.md>\n    11\t\n    12\t## Approach\n    13\t\n    14\tDo NOT read the full diff in one go. Large diffs blow out your context and cause you to stall. Instead, walk the mission's commits one at a time, writing findings to the report file as you go. Keep your working context small by dropping diff text after you have extracted findings from it.\n    15\t\n    16\t## Step 1: Inventory\n    17\t\n    18\tRun:\n    19\t\n    20\t```\n    21\tgit log --reverse --format=\"%h %s\" <base-sha>..HEAD\n    22\t```\n    23\t\n    24\tThis gives you the mission's commits in order. Commits are tagged `[task-N]` in the subject; fix commits are tagged `[task-N] fix:`. Workers run serially, so commits for the same task appear contiguously.\n    25\t\n    26\tInitialize the report file with the header and an empty skeleton:\n    27\t\n    28\t```markdown\n    29\t# Adversarial Review {A|B}\n    30\t\n    31\t## Task 1 -- <commit subject>\n    32\t### Surface summary\n    33\t(changed files + exported symbols/APIs)\n    34\t### Findings\n    35\t(table -- empty is fine)\n    36\t\n    37\t## Task 2 -- <commit subject>\n    38\t...\n    39\t\n    40\t## Cross-task\n    41\t\n    42\t## Summary\n    43\t```\n    44\t\n    45\t## Step 2: Per-commit review loop\n    46\t\n    47\tFor each commit from the inventory, in order:\n    48\t\n    49\t1. Run `git show --stat <sha>` first to see the commit's size and touched files.\n    50\t2. **If the commit is small** (say, under ~600 changed lines and no single file over ~400 lines): read `git show <sha>` in one shot and review it.\n    51\t3. **If the commit is large or touches generated/lock/vendor files:** skip generated/lock/vendor files (`package-lock.json`, `yarn.lock`, `*.min.js`, `dist/**`, `vendor/**`, `__snapshots__/**`, etc.) and iterate the remaining files with `git show <sha> -- <path>` one file at a time. Review each, dropping the diff text between files.\n    52\t4. Review against the contract and the adversarial checklist (below).\n    53\t5. Append to the `## Task N` section of the report:\n    54\t   - A short **Surface summary** listing changed files and any exported symbols/APIs/types/flags introduced or modified. Keep it compact (one line per file). This is the signal the cross-task pass will use.\n    55\t   - A findings table with severity, file:line, and description.\n    56\t   Fix commits append under the same task as the original commit -- do not create separate sections for them.\n    57\t6. **Drop the diff text from your working context before moving to the next commit.** You only need to carry forward a short list of findings so far, not the raw diffs.\n    58\t\n    59\t### Adversarial checklist (apply per commit)\n    60\t\n    61\t- Logic errors or edge cases not covered by tests\n    62\t- Cross-file inconsistencies within this commit (types, interfaces, naming)\n    63\t- Contract assertions that technically pass but have subtle flaws\n    64\t- Security issues, race conditions, or data integrity problems\n    65\t- Missing error handling or graceful degradation\n    66\t- Test doubles leaking into production code paths (NullBridge pattern)\n    67\t- Gaming of contract assertions (smoke test that bypasses real wiring, etc.)\n    68\t\n    69\tFor each finding, classify severity as HIGH, MEDIUM, or LOW. Cite file paths and line numbers.\n    70\t\n    71\t## Step 3: Cross-task pass\n    72\t\n    73\tOnce every commit has been reviewed, re-read the `## Task N` sections of your own report -- both the surface summaries and the findings. Do not re-read the raw diffs. Use the surface summaries to spot interactions the per-commit pass cannot see:\n    74\t\n    75\t- A type or interface introduced in task N consumed incorrectly by task N+k\n    76\t- Race conditions or ordering issues between tasks touching shared state\n    77\t- A contract assertion that no single commit falsifies but the combined behavior does\n    78\t- Integration gaps where task A's outputs do not meet task B's expectations\n    79\t\n    80\tTo verify suspicions, read the **final state** of files from the current working tree (not historical commit blobs). The working tree reflects what will actually ship; commit blobs reflect a point in time that may have been amended by a later fix. Append findings to the `## Cross-task` section.\n    81\t\n    82\t## Step 4: Summary\n    83\t\n    84\tWrite a one-paragraph `## Summary` covering the overall posture of the mission: counts of findings by severity, the biggest risk you see, and whether you would recommend shipping as-is.\n    85\t\n    86\t## Output Format\n    87\t\n    88\tWrite the report to the Report Path. Structure:\n    89\t\n    90\t```markdown\n    91\t# Adversarial Review {A|B}\n    92\t\n    93\t## Task 1 -- <commit subject>\n    94\t| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n    95\t|---|----------|------|---------|\n    96\t| 1 | HIGH | path/to/file.ts:42 | <description> |\n    97\t\n    98\t## Task 2 -- <commit subject>\n    99\t(same table shape; empty table is fine if nothing found)\n   100\t\n   101\t## Cross-task\n   102\t(same table shape)\n   103\t\n   104\t## Summary\n   105\t<one paragraph>\n   106\t```\n   107\t\n   108\tBe adversarial: assume the code is wrong until proven right. Empty sections are fine -- do not invent findings.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
