{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba README.md | sed -n '1,230p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Darkfactory\n     2\t\n     3\t![Darkfactory](docs/darkfactory-header.jpeg)\n     4\t\n     5\tDarkfactory lets you describe what you want to build, and then multiple AI agents plan the work, write the code, check each other's work, and deliver a draft pull request. It runs inside [Claude Code](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code).\n     6\t\n     7\tNo custom IDE or framework required. Darkfactory is built from Claude Code's own features:\n     8\t- **Skills** -- reusable prompt templates you invoke with slash commands (e.g. `/mission`)\n     9\t- **Subagents** -- separate AI workers that each get their own conversation\n    10\t- **Plan mode** -- persistent instructions that survive long sessions\n    11\t- **Markdown files** -- all state lives on disk, not in memory\n    12\t\n    13\tInspired by [Factory's Missions architecture](https://www.factory.ai/) and [Matt Pocock's AI development skills](https://github.com/mattpocock/skills).\n    14\t\n    15\t## Setup\n    16\t\n    17\t### Prerequisites\n    18\t\n    19\t- [Claude Code](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code) installed and authenticated\n    20\t- [OpenSpec CLI](https://github.com/Fission-AI/OpenSpec) installed (`npm install -g openspec`)\n    21\t- [Codex CLI](https://github.com/openai/codex) installed (`npm install -g @openai/codex`) and authenticated (`codex login`)\n    22\t- [GitHub CLI](https://cli.github.com/) installed and authenticated (`gh auth login`) -- used for filing the PRD as a GitHub issue and creating draft PRs\n    23\t- [jq](https://jqlang.github.io/jq/) installed (`brew install jq`) -- used for parsing dispatch results\n    24\t- [Rust toolchain](https://rustup.rs/) installed (`curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh`) -- needed to build darkfactory-dispatch\n    25\t- A git repository to work in\n    26\t\n    27\t### First-time install\n    28\t\n    29\tClone Darkfactory and symlink its skills into your global Claude Code skills directory:\n    30\t\n    31\t```bash\n    32\tgit clone https://github.com/layercodedev/darkfactory.git ~/.darkfactory\n    33\tcd ~/.darkfactory\n    34\t./install.sh\n    35\t```\n    36\t\n    37\tThen build and install the dispatch supervisor:\n    38\t\n    39\t```bash\n    40\tcd ~/.darkfactory/tools/darkfactory-dispatch && make install\n    41\t```\n    42\t\n    43\tThis compiles a Rust binary and copies it to `~/.local/bin/darkfactory-dispatch`. It supervises Codex subprocess dispatches with process group isolation, two-phase timeout, NDJSON heartbeat events, and structured JSON results.\n    44\t\n    45\tSymlinks mean the skills always point to your local clone, so updating is just a `git pull` (see below).\n    46\t\n    47\tRestart Claude Code, then from any project run:\n    48\t\n    49\t```\n    50\t/mission-init\n    51\t```\n    52\t\n    53\tThis checks your entire setup and tells you exactly what's missing. It verifies all 12 skills are symlinked, CLI tools (codex, gh, openspec, darkfactory-dispatch, jq), coverage tools for your project's language (cargo-tarpaulin for Rust, jest/c8 for TypeScript, pytest-cov for Python), and initializes OpenSpec in the current project if needed.\n    54\t\n    55\t### Updating\n    56\t\n    57\tSince skills are symlinked to your clone, pulling updates everything:\n    58\t\n    59\t```bash\n    60\tcd ~/.darkfactory && git pull\n    61\t```\n    62\t\n    63\t## What it does\n    64\t\n    65\tA mission is the core unit of work. It takes you from a goal (\"build user authentication\") to a validated draft PR, through three stages.\n    66\t\n    67\t### Stage 1: You and the orchestrator plan together\n    68\t\n    69\tThe orchestrator is the main Claude Code session that runs your mission. It starts by interviewing you about what you want to build, asking pointed questions until it understands your goals, constraints, and edge cases. It then creates a shared glossary of terms so that you, the orchestrator, and all workers use the same vocabulary.\n    70\t\n    71\tFrom that conversation, it writes a PRD (Product Requirements Document) -- a structured description of the problem, the solution, user stories, and testing approach. The PRD is filed as a GitHub issue and becomes the source of truth for everything that follows. At the end of the mission, the draft PR links back to this issue.\n    72\t\n    73\tNext, it creates a series of planning documents using [OpenSpec](https://github.com/Fission-AI/OpenSpec) (an open-source tool for structured change proposals). These documents are created one at a time, and you review each before the orchestrator moves on:\n    74\t\n    75\t- **Proposal** -- what you're building and why\n    76\t- **Specs** -- detailed requirements written as WHEN/THEN rules (e.g. \"WHEN a user submits the login form with wrong credentials, THEN show an error message\")\n    77\t- **Design** -- architecture decisions and technical approach\n    78\t- **Tasks** -- an ordered checklist of implementation work\n    79\t\n    80\tEvery tasks.md ends with a set of **mandatory closing tasks** that run after the feature work: a smoke test (one end-to-end test through the primary user flow against real dependencies), a cleanup task (remove dead code, unused deps, and leftover scaffolding), a module shape re-check (catch drift between design.md and what the code actually exposes -- required once a mission has 5+ feature tasks), and a documentation task. A CI configuration task is optional and added when relevant.\n    81\t\n    82\tAfter the tasks are defined, the orchestrator runs a **coverage audit**. This maps every requirement from the PRD to one or more tasks. If anything is missing, it flags it. If a requirement was intentionally changed or dropped, you approve that decision explicitly. For substitutions, the orchestrator also checks that the replacement approach is fully covered by existing tasks -- if the substitution drops something important, it's flagged as a gap. Nothing falls through the cracks by accident.\n    83\t\n    84\tFinally, it writes a **contract** -- a checklist of things the finished code should do, written so they can be tested from the outside (by running the code, not by reading the source). For example: \"the login endpoint returns a 401 when given wrong credentials.\" Each item traces back to a PRD requirement and is tagged with how it should be verified (by executing the code, checking its output, or inspecting the source). Before moving on, the orchestrator runs a testability check to catch any vague or untestable items early. You review the contract, confirm which AI models handle each role (see [Configuration](#configuration)), and approve launch.\n    85\t\n    86\t### Stage 2: The orchestrator builds and validates autonomously\n    87\t\n    88\tFor each task, the orchestrator sends a worker (Codex or Claude, your choice) a brief describing what to build. The worker writes the code, commits its changes, and writes a handoff report. Workers never see the contract -- this is intentional, and explained in [information walls](#information-walls) below.\n    89\t\n    90\tAfter each worker finishes, two validators check the work:\n    91\t\n    92\t1. **Scrutiny validator** -- a thorough quality check that runs a sequence of checks:\n    93\t   - Do tests exist? (auto-fails the task if not)\n    94\t   - Does the project build, do the tests pass, does the linter pass?\n    95\t   - Code review and code standards check\n    96\t   - Module depth review -- did the change make modules shallower or leak internal details?\n    97\t   - Complexity check -- is the code more complex than it needs to be?\n    98\t   - Security review (only when the changes touch auth, secrets, user input, network boundaries, or filesystem trust boundaries)\n    99\t   - Contract check -- does the code do what the contract says it should?\n   100\t   - Execution check -- actually runs the code to confirm it works, rather than just reading the source\n   101\t   - Test health checks -- silent skip detection, diff coverage on changed lines, and test quality review\n   102\t\n   103\t2. **User-test validator** -- tests the feature the way a user would. It decides whether to hit an API with HTTP requests, drive a browser, or both, based on what the task changed.\n   104\t\n   105\tIf either validator finds problems, the orchestrator sends a fix worker a description of what went wrong. It only describes the symptoms -- it never reveals the contract, so the fix worker has to solve the real problem, not just game the checklist. After the fix, both validators re-run from scratch.\n   106\t\n   107\tThis loop repeats until the task passes or hits the **circuit breaker**. After 3 total attempts (1 initial build + 2 fix rounds), the orchestrator pauses and asks you: retry, skip this task, or abort the mission.\n   108\t\n   109\t### Stage 3: You review and merge\n   110\t\n   111\tAfter all tasks are complete, the orchestrator runs three final checks:\n   112\t\n   113\t1. **Full-repo build check** -- builds, tests, lints, and formats the entire repository (not just the changed files). This catches cases where a late task accidentally broke something an earlier task built.\n   114\t2. **Requirements check** -- compares the built code against the coverage audit. Did you actually build everything you said you would? If something is missing, you decide whether to add a fix task, reclassify it as intentionally skipped, or accept the gap.\n   115\t3. **Independent code review** -- two separate reviewers (Codex and Claude, running at the same time) each review all the changes against the full contract. Their reports are presented to you alongside the draft PR link. This is not an automated gate -- you read both reports and decide what to address.\n   116\t\n   117\tYou review the findings, promote the PR from draft to ready, and merge.\n   118\t\n   119\t## Architecture\n   120\t\n   121\t![Darkfactory architecture diagram](docs/architecture.png)\n   122\t\n   123\t### Three roles\n   124\t\n   125\tThe system has three roles, each handled by a separate AI agent:\n   126\t\n   127\t**Orchestrator** -- the main Claude Code session you interact with. It plans the work, sends out one worker at a time, reads their results, runs validators, and decides what to do next. It uses Claude Code's \"plan mode,\" which keeps its instructions alive even when the conversation gets long enough that older messages are dropped to save space.\n   128\t\n   129\t**Workers** -- each worker builds a single task. By default, workers are powered by Codex (OpenAI's coding agent), but you can use Claude instead. Codex workers are dispatched through `darkfactory-dispatch`, a Rust process supervisor that provides process group isolation, two-phase timeout (SIGTERM then SIGKILL), heartbeat events every 15 seconds, and structured JSON results. Claude workers use Claude Code's built-in Agent tool. A worker gets a brief with everything it needs: what to build, how the project is structured, coding rules to follow, which files to look at, and a summary of what previous tasks already built (file paths, function names, and types -- facts only, never reasoning from other workers). It makes its changes in a single commit and writes a report describing what it did. Workers never see the contract -- they only know what the brief tells them.\n   130\t\n   131\t**Validators** -- after each worker finishes, validators check the work against the contract. Two validators run back-to-back for every task: the scrutiny validator (a thorough quality check) and the user-test validator (which tests the feature the way a real user would). Validators never see the proposal or design documents -- they only see the code and the contract.\n   132\t\n   133\t### Information walls\n   134\t\n   135\tWorkers never see the contract. Validators never see the design. Why?\n   136\t\n   137\tIf a validator could see the design intent, it might look at the code and think \"this looks like what the design described\" without actually checking whether the code works. By keeping workers and validators blind to each other's information, the system forces validation to be based on what the code *does*, not what someone *intended* it to do.\n   138\t\n   139\tThe same principle applies when fixing problems. When a validator finds something wrong, the orchestrator tells the fix worker what the symptoms are -- but never reveals the contract. The fix worker has to solve the real problem, not just make a checklist item pass.\n   140\t\n   141\t### Fix loop\n   142\t\n   143\tWhen either validator fails a task, a diagnostic triage agent first reads the failure report and the code changes to pinpoint the likely root cause (which file, which line, what went wrong). The orchestrator then sends a fix worker the symptoms and triage findings -- but never the contract, so the information wall is maintained. After the fix, both validators re-run from scratch. If it fails again, another fix round runs.\n   144\t\n   145\tThe **circuit breaker** stops this from going forever. After 3 total attempts (the first build plus 2 fix rounds), the orchestrator pauses and asks you: retry (the counter keeps going -- it doesn't reset), skip this task, or abort the mission.\n   146\t\n   147\t### File-backed state\n   148\t\n   149\tAll mission state lives in files on disk, not in memory. This has two practical benefits: if your Claude Code session crashes or you close the terminal, you can resume the mission from where it left off. And you can open a second terminal to check progress while a mission is running.\n   150\t\n   151\t```\n   152\topenspec/changes/<change-name>/\n   153\t  proposal.md              # What and why\n   154\t  specs/<capability>/spec.md  # Formal WHEN/THEN requirements (one per capability)\n   155\t  design.md                # How (architecture decisions)\n   156\t  tasks.md                 # Task checklist (includes mandatory closing tasks)\n   157\t  coverage-audit.md        # PRD requirement-to-task mapping\n   158\t  contract.md              # What the code should do (traced to PRD, tagged by verification method)\n   159\t  mission/\n   160\t    config.yaml            # Engine settings per role\n   161\t    progress.md            # Orchestrator's running log\n   162\t    briefs/task-N.md       # Worker briefs\n   163\t    handoffs/task-N.md     # Worker handoff reports\n   164\t    validations/           # Validator reports, demo videos\n   165\t    issues/                # Failure details per attempt\n   166\t    logs/                  # Streaming output from workers and reviewers (tail -f to watch live)\n   167\t```\n   168\t\n   169\t## Skills\n   170\t\n   171\tDarkfactory is controlled through skills -- reusable prompt templates you invoke with a slash command in Claude Code.\n   172\t\n   173\t| Skill | Purpose |\n   174\t|-------|---------|\n   175\t| `/mission` | Start an autonomous mission from scratch. Walks you through planning, dispatches workers, validates, and delivers a draft PR. |\n   176\t| `/mission-resume` | Resume a mission that was interrupted (session crash, closed terminal). Detects exactly where it left off and continues through to the draft PR and code review. |\n   177\t| `/mission-status` | Read-only progress check. Open a second terminal while a mission runs to see which tasks are done, in progress, or queued. |\n   178\t| `/mission-init` | Check your project setup. Verifies all 12 skill symlinks, CLI tools, and coverage tools. Runs `openspec init` if needed. |\n   179\t| `/grill` | Alignment interview: asks pointed questions until the goals, constraints, and edge cases are clear. Used in the mission planning phase. |\n   180\t| `/prd` | Turns an interview into a structured PRD filed as a GitHub issue. Used in the mission planning phase. |\n   181\t| `/language` | Creates a shared glossary (ubiquitous language) so you, the orchestrator, and all workers use the same terms. Used in the mission planning phase. |\n   182\t| `/tdd` | Test-driven development guidance. Workers invoke this during the build phase. Companion files cover deep modules, interfaces, mocking, refactoring, and test patterns. |\n   183\t| `/test-quality` | LLM-based semantic review of test effectiveness. The scrutiny validator runs this as a subagent after every worker commit. |\n   184\t| `/security` | Security review. The scrutiny validator runs this when the diff touches auth, secrets, user input, or network/filesystem boundaries. |\n   185\t| `/adversarial` | Dual-agent code review: dispatches a Codex reviewer and a Claude reviewer with identical input, then surfaces disagreements. |\n   186\t| `/dispatch` | Run a coding task via `darkfactory-dispatch`. Accepts a task description inline or prompts for one. Presents structured outcome. |\n   187\t\n   188\t### Skill structure\n   189\t\n   190\t`/mission` is split across files so only the control flow is always in context:\n   191\t\n   192\t- `SKILL.md` -- control flow, rules, phase gates (auto-loaded)\n   193\t- `templates/` -- artifacts the orchestrator fills in: `plan.md`, `coverage-audit.md`, `contract.md`, `worker-brief.md`, `worker-fix-brief.md`, `pr-body.md`\n   194\t- `prompts/` -- prompts handed to subagents: `scrutiny-validator.md`, `usertest-validator.md`, `spec-delta-checker.md`, `adversarial-reviewer.md`\n   195\t\n   196\tCompanion files are read on demand when the skill reaches the step that needs them.\n   197\t\n   198\t### Other skills used by `/mission`\n   199\t\n   200\tAll Darkfactory skills are bundled in this repo. `/mission` also calls two built-in Claude Code skills and accepts two optional project-specific skills:\n   201\t\n   202\t| Skill | Source | When it's used |\n   203\t|-------|--------|---------|\n   204\t| `/review` | Built-in Claude Code (required) | Scrutiny validator: code review step |\n   205\t| `/simplify` | Built-in Claude Code (required) | Scrutiny validator: complexity check step |\n   206\t| `/test-app` | Your project's Claude skills (optional) | User-test validator: browser-based testing |\n   207\t| `/demo-video` | Your project's Claude skills (optional) | Records a demo of frontend changes after validation passes |\n   208\t| `openspec` CLI | Installed by `npm install -g openspec` | Planning phase: creates the structured artifacts (proposal, specs, design, tasks) |\n   209\t| `/opsx:archive` | Bundled with Darkfactory | Post-merge: moves the change's planning files into the OpenSpec archive directory |\n   210\t\n   211\t`/review` and `/simplify` are mandatory -- the scrutiny validator always runs them. `/test-app` and `/demo-video` are optional and project-specific -- you write these yourself for your project if you want browser testing or demo recordings.\n   212\t\n   213\t### Configuration\n   214\t\n   215\tEach mission creates a `config.yaml` that controls which AI engine handles each role. You can change these before approving the plan:\n   216\t\n   217\t```yaml\n   218\tengines:\n   219\t  worker: codex                    # or claude\n   220\t  scrutiny_validator: claude\n   221\t  usertest_validator: claude\n   222\t  adversarial_reviewer_a: codex\n   223\t  adversarial_reviewer_b: claude\n   224\t  demo_video: claude\n   225\t```\n   226\t\n   227\tThe default uses Codex for workers and one of the two independent code reviewers, and Claude for everything else. You can set any role to either engine.\n   228\t\n   229\t## Usage\n   230\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
