{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_73","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' openspec/changes/skill-decomposition/mission/validations/adversarial-a.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Adversarial Review A\n\n## Task 1 -- Add skill-decomposition OpenSpec artifacts and coverage audit\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 2 -- Add mission directory, contract, and coverage audit for skill-decomposition\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 3 -- [task-1] move mission skills to skills/ at repo root\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 4 -- [task-1a] update mission SKILL.md skill references to new names\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 5 -- [task-1a] fix: add /mission-init call to Phase 0\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 6 -- [task-2] fork grill, prd, language skills into skills/\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 7 -- [task-2] fix: add user-invokable: true to grill, prd, language frontmatter\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 8 -- [task-3] fork tdd and test-quality skills, update scrutiny-validator ref\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 9 -- [task-4] fork security skill into skills/\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 10 -- [task-5] write mission-init skill\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 11 -- [task-6] write adversarial skill\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n| 1 | HIGH | skills/adversarial/SKILL.md:70 | The Codex dispatch example passes both `--async` and `--json`. `darkfactory-dispatch` defines these flags as mutually exclusive (`tools/darkfactory-dispatch/src/cli.rs:99` and `:102`) and has a regression test expecting exit code 2 for this exact combination (`tools/darkfactory-dispatch/tests/cli.rs:67`). Following the skill's own instructions will fail before the Codex reviewer starts, so the standalone `/adversarial` workflow does not actually work. |\n\n## Task 12 -- [task-7] write dispatch skill\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 13 -- [task-8] create install.sh\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n| 1 | MEDIUM | install.sh:18 | `rm -f \"$target\"` does not replace an existing real directory at `~/.claude/skills/<name>`. In that common upgrade case, `ln -sf \"$skill_dir\" \"$target\"` creates a nested symlink inside the stale directory, exits 0, and leaves `~/.claude/skills/<name>` as a directory instead of the required symlink. I reproduced this with a temp `HOME`: precreating `.claude/skills/mission` left it as a directory containing `mission/mission`, while the script still exited 0. |\n\n## Task 14 -- [task-8] guard empty skills dir and POSIX glob edge case\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n| 1 | MEDIUM | install.sh:14 | The loop iterates `\"$SKILLS_DIR\"/*/`, so each symlink target includes a trailing slash. `readlink ~/.claude/skills/mission` therefore returns `.../.darkfactory/skills/mission/`, while `/mission-init` later compares against `.../.darkfactory/skills/mission` exactly. A clean install can be reported as \"wrong target\" by the verifier. |\n\n## Task 15 -- [task-9] update README skills table and external skills section\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 16 -- [task-c4] fix stale doc references\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Task 17 -- [task-c2] cleanup old skill refs and remove dead skill directories\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n\n## Cross-task\n| # | Severity | File | Finding |\n|---|----------|------|---------|\n| 1 | HIGH | skills/mission-init/SKILL.md:47 | `install.sh` and `/mission-init` disagree on the exact symlink target. The installer writes targets with a trailing slash because of `for skill_dir in \"$SKILLS_DIR\"/*/`, but `/mission-init` requires exact string equality with `$HOME/.darkfactory/skills/$skill`. Running the installer with a temp `HOME` produced `readlink .../mission` = `/tmp/.../.darkfactory/skills/mission/`; the verifier would classify that as `[!] /mission - wrong target`. This breaks the documented first-run flow: install, restart, run `/mission-init`. |\n| 2 | MEDIUM | skills/mission-init/SKILL.md:91 | The OpenSpec install command is inconsistent across files. README says `npm install -g openspec` (`README.md:20`, `README.md:208`), but `/mission-init` tells users to run `npm install -g @fission-ai/openspec` (`skills/mission-init/SKILL.md:91`, `:120`). A user following setup docs and then verifier remediation may install a different package name or get conflicting advice; one of these references is stale. |\n| 3 | LOW | .codex/skills/openspec-apply-change/SKILL.md | Task C2 removed tracked `.codex/skills` files, so `git ls-files .codex/skills/` passes, but the working tree still contains untracked `.codex/skills/openspec-*` directories. They are not in the PR, but the cleanup goal was \"no .codex/skills duplicates\"; a local mission run after this cleanup still sees duplicate skill files on disk. |\n\n## Summary\nOverall posture: not ready to ship as-is. I found 2 HIGH, 3 MEDIUM, and 1 LOW issues. The biggest risk is setup breakage: `install.sh` can either leave stale directories in place or create symlink targets that `/mission-init` rejects, so the promised single install + verifier path is not reliable. The second blocker is `/adversarial` documenting an invalid `darkfactory-dispatch --async --json` command, which prevents the Codex reviewer from starting in the standalone skill. The skill names themselves mostly line up, and the old-name grep checks pass inside `skills/`, but I would fix the installer/verifier contract and the adversarial dispatch flags before shipping.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
